145
00:06:16.829 --> 00:06:20.019
Now that you know where you might encounter salary ranges as a manager,

146
00:06:20.269 --> 00:06:24.570
let's take a closer look at an important factor that influences salary ranges.

147
00:06:25.299 --> 00:06:26.799
Work location.

148
00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:29.890
Our roles market-based salary range is influenced by the

149
00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.579
location in which the work is taking place.

150
00:06:31.929 --> 00:06:34.600
Stanford supports flexible work and enables remote work

151
00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.779
if it is appropriate for the specific job.

152
00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.859
To make this approach equitable across the university.

153
00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.230
Compensation must be considered.

154
00:06:42.369 --> 00:06:44.890
This means that the employee's work location factors

155
00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:47.209
into the appropriate salary range for their role,

156
00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:49.790
which is known as a location-based pay approach.

157
00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:51.970
In addition to the factors already shared,

158
00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:55.450
Stanford's benchmarking includes a market analysis of the Bay Area

159
00:06:55.609 --> 00:06:57.910
and other geographic areas across the country.

160
00:06:58.089 --> 00:07:00.929
This informs the specific salary range for an employee

161
00:07:00.929 --> 00:07:02.829
based on where they are doing their work.

162
00:07:03.149 --> 00:07:07.269
Supports equity and consistency across our workforce because it factors in the

163
00:07:07.269 --> 00:07:10.850
cost of labor in the specific location where the work is performed.

164
00:07:11.029 --> 00:07:14.190
This approach allows us to pay employees appropriately for work

165
00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.429
performed on site or hybrid from a Stanford work location,

166
00:07:17.779 --> 00:07:20.140
meaning one of Stanford's offices or buildings,

167
00:07:20.429 --> 00:07:21.989
and for work performed remotely.

168
00:07:22.269 --> 00:07:23.170
As a manager,

169
00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.950
it is important to keep Stanford's location based.

170
00:07:26.089 --> 00:07:27.149
Pay approach in mind

171
00:07:27.489 --> 00:07:29.179
when hiring a remote employee

172
00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:31.880
or when a current employee moves work locations.

173
00:07:32.019 --> 00:07:34.070
This has an impact on the appropriate

174
00:07:34.339 --> 00:07:36.829
market-based salary range for that employee.

175
00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:40.160
Your HR partners will work with you to ensure the correct

176
00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.950
location-based salary range is reflected in any remote employee job offer

177
00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.049
and to ensure that necessary adjustments are

178
00:07:46.049 --> 00:07:47.950
made following an employee's change in work,

179
00:07:48.519 --> 00:07:48.769
location.

180
00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.989
While you do not need to be a compensation expert,

181
00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:53.820
it will be helpful for you to understand

182
00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.529
the basics of Stanford's location-based pay approach,

183
00:07:56.779 --> 00:07:58.450
so you can explain it to your employees.

184
00:07:58.579 --> 00:08:00.239
A few important points include,

185
00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:03.540
Stanford uses a 5 zone model based on city,

186
00:08:03.829 --> 00:08:04.179
state,

187
00:08:04.309 --> 00:08:05.230
and zip code

188
00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:06.910
of where the work will be done.

189
00:08:07.149 --> 00:08:08.940
Over 42,000 cities,

190
00:08:09.269 --> 00:08:09.709
states,

191
00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:12.429
zip codes are grouped into these 5 zones.

192
00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:16.190
Each zone uses a geographic differential to adjust the pay range,

193
00:08:16.299 --> 00:08:20.369
making it market accurate and consistent across all jobs in the same zip code.

194
00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.589
A geographic differential considers the work location,

195
00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:26.089
reflecting differences in cost of labor.

196
00:08:26.489 --> 00:08:30.690
The geographic differential is based on how the zone's pay compares to zone one.

197
00:08:30.929 --> 00:08:33.549
Zone one includes all 10 Bay Area Counties,

198
00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:37.130
often referred to as Stanford Work location or SWL

199
00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:40.239
and serves as the point of comparison for the other four zones.

200
00:08:40.479 --> 00:08:44.000
This means zone one has a geographic differential of 100%.

201
00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:45.150
In comparison,

202
00:08:45.289 --> 00:08:48.340
zone 2 has a geographic differential of 90%

203
00:08:48.729 --> 00:08:53.169
because it includes locations where the cost of labor is 85 to 94% of zone one.

204
00:08:53.369 --> 00:08:57.309
The geographic differential is the average cost of labor within the specific zone.

205
00:08:57.849 --> 00:09:00.710
Zone 3 has a geographic differential of 80%

206
00:09:00.969 --> 00:09:05.429
and includes locations where the cost of labor is 75 to 84% of zone one.

207
00:09:05.659 --> 00:09:08.590
Zone 4 has a 70% geographic differential.

208
00:09:09.119 --> 00:09:14.059
And includes locations where the cost of labor is 65 to 74% of zone one.

209
00:09:14.239 --> 00:09:15.109
And finally,

210
00:09:15.349 --> 00:09:18.539
zone 5 has a 60% geographic differential

211
00:09:18.679 --> 00:09:23.359
and includes locations where the cost of labor is 55 to 64% of zone one.

212
00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.140
Let's look at an example.

213
00:09:25.599 --> 00:09:29.159
Let's say that you are hiring an administrative Services administrator 1

214
00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.390
and you have 5 qualified candidates,

215
00:09:31.719 --> 00:09:34.469
one from each of Stanford's location-based pay zones.

216
00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:38.080
The salary range that is appropriate for each candidate depends

217
00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:39.979
on the zone in which they will be working.

218
00:09:40.159 --> 00:09:40.940
For example,

219
00:09:41.119 --> 00:09:44.000
a mid-range offer to the candidate in Palo Alto

220
00:09:44.159 --> 00:09:47.270
would be higher than a mid-range offer to the candidate working from Salem,

221
00:09:47.349 --> 00:09:48.000
Arkansas.

222
00:09:48.159 --> 00:09:49.380
As you can see from the table,

223
00:09:49.679 --> 00:09:50.640
the midpoint of this.

224
00:09:50.739 --> 00:09:53.289
Same job working in each zone is adjusted by the

225
00:09:53.289 --> 00:09:57.239
specific percentage that reflects the work location's geographic differential.

226
00:09:57.419 --> 00:09:58.989
While it is important to understand

227
00:09:58.989 --> 00:10:01.659
Stanford's location-based pay approach and the

228
00:10:01.659 --> 00:10:05.210
impact of an employee's work location on the applicable salary range,

229
00:10:05.419 --> 00:10:07.719
you do not need to do this calculation yourself.

230
00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.450
Your HR partners will support you in

231
00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.940
making appropriate salary offers to new employees.

232
00:10:12.049 --> 00:10:12.690
Based on their work,

233
00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:13.349
location,

234
00:10:13.369 --> 00:10:14.320
and other factors.

235
00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:16.479
They will also support you if any adjustments

236
00:10:16.479 --> 00:10:18.559
are needed when a current employee moves.

237
00:10:18.809 --> 00:10:21.950
Please keep in mind that Stanford's compensation policy

238
00:10:22.169 --> 00:10:25.429
states that pay should reflect the location-based pay range

239
00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:27.510
within the employees assigned zone.

240
00:10:27.679 --> 00:10:31.590
All pay decisions should be guided by fairness and equity for all employees

241
00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.309
and aligned to Stanford's policies.